<header class="ws-head"><p class="ws-kicker">Worksheet &middot; Reading path</p><h1 class="ws-title">Worksheet: Voyagers of the Pacific</h1><p class="ws-lede">Canoes, stars and sacred kings: the peoples who settled the widest ocean on Earth, and what happened when Europe arrived.</p><p class="ws-who" aria-hidden="true"><span>Name</span><span>Class</span><span>Date</span></p><p class="ws-tools"><button type="button" class="ws-print" data-key="0">Print the worksheet</button><button type="button" class="ws-print" data-key="1">Print with the answers</button><a href="/paths/pacific-voyagers">Open the path &rarr;</a></p></header>
<section class="ws-task"><h2><span class="ws-n">1</span>Put the stops in order</h2><p class="ws-how">Number the stops from 1 (earliest) to 6 (latest), by when each began: a state&#8217;s founding, a person&#8217;s birth, the date of an event.</p><ul class="ws-order"><li><span class="ws-box" aria-hidden="true"></span><span>Kingdom of Hawaii</span></li><li><span class="ws-box" aria-hidden="true"></span><span>Rapa Nui</span></li><li><span class="ws-box" aria-hidden="true"></span><span>Saudeleur dynasty</span></li><li><span class="ws-box" aria-hidden="true"></span><span>Treaty of Waitangi</span></li><li><span class="ws-box" aria-hidden="true"></span><span>Tupaia</span></li><li><span class="ws-box" aria-hidden="true"></span><span>Tuʻi Tonga Empire</span></li></ul></section>
<section class="ws-task"><h2><span class="ws-n">2</span>Think and discuss</h2><ol class="ws-qs"><li><p>Stop 1, <b>Polynesians</b>. The path begins here because: &#8220;Descended from Austronesian seafarers of the Lapita culture, they reached Tonga and Samoa around 800 BCE and later found the remotest islands by stars, winds and swells.&#8221; What does this stop tell you about how the story began?</p><span class="ws-lines" aria-hidden="true"><span></span><span></span><span></span></span></li><li><p>Stop 5, <b>Tupaia</b>: &#8220;The Raiatean priest and navigator who joined Cook's Endeavour in 1769, drew a chart of the islands Tahitians knew and spoke for Cook with the Māori.&#8221; Find one detail on its page that supports this, and one question it leaves open.</p><span class="ws-lines" aria-hidden="true"><span></span><span></span><span></span></span></li><li><p>Stop 9, <b>Kingdom of Tonga</b>: &#8220;The only Pacific island state never formally colonised: the kingdom George Tupou I founded in 1845, whose constitution of 1875 is still in force.&#8221; How does this last stop connect to an earlier one?</p><span class="ws-lines" aria-hidden="true"><span></span><span></span><span></span></span></li><li><p>The path sums itself up as: &#8220;Canoes, stars and sacred kings: the peoples who settled the widest ocean on Earth, and what happened when Europe arrived.&#8221; Which stop shows this best? Explain your choice.</p><span class="ws-lines" aria-hidden="true"><span></span><span></span><span></span></span></li></ol></section>
<p class="ws-src">From The Epoch Institute: the-epoch-institute.org/paths/pacific-voyagers. Free to print and copy for use in class.</p>
<details class="ws-key" id="answers"><summary>Answer key</summary><div class="ws-key-b"><p class="ws-key-t">Answer key: Worksheet: Voyagers of the Pacific</p><section><h2><span class="ws-n">1</span>Put the stops in order</h2><ol class="ws-key-list"><li>Tuʻi Tonga Empire <span>from 950 CE</span></li><li>Saudeleur dynasty <span>from 1100 CE</span></li><li>Rapa Nui <span>from 1200 CE</span></li><li>Tupaia <span>born c. 1725 CE</span></li><li>Kingdom of Hawaii <span>from 1795 CE</span></li><li>Treaty of Waitangi <span>1840</span></li></ol></section><section><h2><span class="ws-n">2</span>Think and discuss</h2><p class="ws-key-note">Open questions: answers will vary. Good answers use details from the pages of the path.</p></section></div></details>
